From cb168f79e4807c2744c6bc276db6dd5fcbd18e96 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Angel Martinez <110217098+anmartinez1256@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 12:17:42 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 06/39] Review fixes: probe end delays and mode match, =0
 switches, FTZ restore, size guard

Fixes from a further AI-assisted read-only review of the fork, checked on the Apple M5
as listed at the end.

Probe end criterion (OPENEMS_PROBE_END_DB):
- The excitation counts as running until its signal length plus the largest excitation
  delay: the voltage and current entries of every grid (also the inner grids of a
  cylindrical multigrid) and the TFSF propagation delay. Before, a port excited with a
  delay could let the run stop before its pulse had started.
- Mode-match (waveguide port) probes count with their amplitude only
  (ProcessIntegral::GetNumberOfSignalIntegrals()). Their second row, the mode purity,
  stays near 1 while any field is there and kept the criterion from ever firing.
- A probe whose values are all still exactly zero holds the run, as before. Once every
  other probe has settled for the hold time, a one-time notice on stderr names it.
- OPENEMS_PROBE_END_DB unset, empty or 0 is off without a message; a positive,
  unparsable or non-finite value prints a warning and leaves the criterion off.
  OPENEMS_PROBE_END_HOLD unset or empty is 1 excitation length and 0 is no hold; a
  negative, unparsable or non-finite value prints a warning and uses 1. The hold is
  clamped to UINT_MAX timesteps.
- With valid values and without excitation delays, TFSF or mode-match probes the stop
  step is unchanged.

Switches: OPENEMS_FTZ, OPENEMS_NO_STAGGER, OPENEMS_NO_PACKED_COEFF, OPENEMS_NO_PML_TABLE,
OPENEMS_NF2FF_LEGACY and OPENEMS_SETUP_TIMING act only when set to a value other than
empty or "0" (EnvFlag() in tools/useful.h; the public allocator.h repeats the test).
Before, NAME=0 turned them on. OPENEMS_FUSED=0, OPENEMS_BALANCE=0, OPENEMS_QOS,
OPENEMS_TUNE_WINDOW_MS and OPENEMS_BARRIER_SPIN_US keep their meaning, but an empty
value now counts as unset for them too (before, it read as 0: no fused sweep, no slab
balancing, the default QoS, a zero tuning window, no spinning). So for every variable
of the fork, an empty value is the same as an unset one.

Flush to zero (arm64, OPENEMS_FTZ): Engine_sse saves the FPCR.FZ bit of the thread that
creates it and restores it when it is destroyed there. The Python thread keeps its
subnormals after a run, and a later basic-engine run in the same process no longer
flushes. x86 keeps MXCSR FZ|DAZ on the creating thread, as in stock. The basic engine
never flushes (now said in denormal.h).

Packed coefficient tables: the size test is gone. It could only fire through z padding,
and then it also turned off the fused sweep. With at most one operator per z-vector the
tables never exceed twice the field memory, so they are always built. Only a failed
allocation skips them: both are then freed, and the multithreaded engine says "no fused
sweep, no packed coefficient tables" (verbose > 0).

Multithreaded engine:
- Auto thread tuner: the start count and every candidate (also start-2 and the
  refinement) are clamped to the threads the operator can give x-lines to
  (CalcStartStopLines), so no window re-times a clamped count.
- The fused-sweep line prints only when it changes, one at a time: the level engines of
  a cylindrical multigrid printed it twice each, from several threads at once (a data
  race on cout under TSan). A new line says when there are fewer x-line blocks than
  threads.

32-bit sizes: SetupFDTD refuses, with error code 2 and a message, a mesh that the 32-bit
array sizes and indices (ArrayLib, AdrOp) cannot address, instead of writing past a
wrapped array: at most 1431655765 nodes when a three-component array spans the mesh
(field dumps and nf2ff boxes through the stored material, conducting sheets, the basic
and GPU engines, the debug dumps), 4294967295 otherwise, also for the f4vector arrays.

nf2ff: only <vecLib/cblas.h> instead of the Accelerate umbrella header, which GCC cannot
parse. It keeps the older CBLAS interface on purpose: the newer one
(ACCELERATE_NEW_LAPACK) rounds differently and changes the far field in the last bits.
The call silences its deprecation warning. The blocks of directions now also bound the
P buffer: G and P stay within 2^21 complex floats each (a block keeps at least 16
directions), and the block size is unchanged where NPP <= 6*NP.

Raw export (OPENEMS_EXPORT_OPERATOR, OPENEMS_EXPORT_FIELDS): every failed write or close
is reported, and the last line then says that the export is incomplete instead of
"exported". A warning gives the number of current excitation entries, which are not
exported. An empty value exports nothing (before, it tried to write the files to /).
The files written are byte-identical to before.

Portability and comments: the spin barrier emits the yield hint only on ARM targets that
have it (ARMv6K and later, ARMv6-M); nf2ff_calc.cpp includes <algorithm>; the Mur line
hooks lost an unused variable. Comments corrected: GetCoupledLines (also hooks that read
x-1), the Mur f4vector lanes, the cache-line padding (64 B; the M5's line is 128 B), the
QoS wording, the excitation's barrier saving (unfused plan only), the allocator stagger
(see the 0003 correction below), the tuner's P (Apple only), the nf2ff block comment and its
unreachable fallback, the x86-only TODO in denormal.h; review dates and a private path
removed.

Not changed: the operator setup uses every core whatever numThreads says. Bit identity
with stock v0.37.0 holds for the same number of timesteps: the energy end criterion keeps
upstream's schedule (eb1c5ee), so runs that end on it stop at other steps than v0.37.0.
The bit-identity checks used Apple clang builds; with GCC, its default contraction
(-ffp-contract=fast: x86 with FMA, and arm64) can round differently from stock in the
last bit. The library ABI differs from v0.37.0 (ArrayBase::m_owner from upstream
gpu-engine, the allocator stagger), so both Python modules (CSXCAD and openEMS) must be
rebuilt against it after it is installed over another build, or SAR goes wrong.
Upstream fixes after the base that this branch does not have: 367a63f (flush to zero
during the run only, every engine), 54a4c60 (setup and run errors caught instead of
terminating), d1fef7c (no excitation before its delay), 0212258c3 (sizes and counts for
large meshes), c971899 (an unknown --engine is an error), 7badca4 (NrTS limited to
INT_MAX), ec4d2db (an invalid command line option is reported), dd72017 (Write2XML on
an incomplete setup), 5c8053e (SAR dump without a second copy of the frequency domain
fields).

Verified on the Apple M5, at most 2 engine threads (auto where noted):
- No switches set: bit-identical to the build of 99e20fd on the patch tutorial and the
  GCPW line, 3000 timesteps, 2 threads and auto: port files and et/ht after their version
  line, HDF5 dumps. GPU engine on the patch tutorial: the same.
- Debye box (60x60x20, 600 steps): basic, sse-compressed and multithreaded bit-identical.
- Each switch: unset, empty and 0 behave the same and 1 acts. OPENEMS_SETUP_TIMING,
  OPENEMS_NO_PACKED_COEFF, OPENEMS_NO_PML_TABLE and OPENEMS_NO_STAGGER leave the fields of
  a box with a six-sided PML bit-identical. OPENEMS_NF2FF_LEGACY=1 gives the legacy far
  field of the build of 99e20fd. OPENEMS_NO_STAGGER=1 removes the allocation offsets.
  OPENEMS_FTZ=1 flushes (no subnormals left in the fields of a 241x33x33 box after 300
  steps, against 44529 without), the Python thread keeps its subnormals afterwards, and
  a basic-engine run after it in the same process is bit-identical to one without it.
  An empty OPENEMS_FUSED, OPENEMS_BALANCE, OPENEMS_QOS or OPENEMS_BARRIER_SPIN_US keeps
  the fused sweep, OPENEMS_FUSED=0 turns it off; the fields and far field of that box
  stay bit-identical in every case.
- Probe end: two lumped ports, the second excited 8.594e-9 s late: the run now stops
  after 7434 timesteps with port 2's full peak (3381 before, ahead of its pulse). A WR-90
  section with two mode-match ports stops after 4636 timesteps (ran to its 60000
  before). A TFSF plane wave counts until timestep 428 (signal 372 + delay 56) and stops
  after 798 timesteps (750 before). A probe on a PEC strip prints the notice. Without
  delays the stop steps are the same as before (2961, 1491 and 2226 for hold 1, 0 and
  0.5; 4056 for a Mur box with RLC loads). The stopped port files are a prefix of the
  full run's.
- nf2ff: the separable and legacy results are bit-identical to the build of 99e20fd, the
  separable one also on a thin box where the new bound splits the blocks.
- Size guard: 1200x1200x1050 with a field dump or the basic engine, and 1700x1700x1500,
  are refused at setup.
- Cylindrical multigrid: the fused-sweep line once per level engine (3 lines, 6 before);
  the outputs are bit-identical.

Corrections to earlier commit messages (not reworded). The snapshot
names in them (cg9 to cg17) are emsKit's build snapshots; the GCPW line and the SMA
launch are the two board models of the Kirchhoff rfsim toolkit that emsKit benchmarks.
- 0001 (9651fff): "bit-identical to stock" holds for the same number of timesteps (see
  "Not changed" above). P in the tuner search is the performance-core count on Apple
  only; elsewhere the search starts at half the available threads. "The step-2 CPU work
  and this session's items" means the CPU engine changes listed in that message.
- 0002 (4acfb64): the setup times (4.1 -> 0.9 s, 4.1 -> 1.3 s) compare stock with
  numThreads=4, whose setup then runs on 4 threads, with this build, whose setup uses
  every core (10 on the M5). nf2ff runs one cgemm per block of directions on each plane,
  not one per plane. The raw operator export writes the voltage excitation entries only.
  OPENEMS_FTZ=1 flushes in the SSE-family engines (on the thread that creates the engine
  and in the multithreaded workers), not in the basic engine, and until this commit the
  creating thread kept the flag after the run.
- 0003 (487e684): the 3-4x slowdown of 0.2-0.3 Mcell models is a one-thread figure
  (3.5-3.6x for 0.200-0.338 Mcells with 20 z-lines), and it is not cache-set aliasing:
  offsets of 16 and 32 KB remove it as well as 256 B-4 KB do (the opposite result came
  from single runs and does not reproduce). It appears when macOS 27's allocator places
  the two field arrays, 2-4 MiB each (about 0.175-0.35 Mcells), exactly 4 MiB + 16 KiB
  apart; the hardware mechanism was not identified. The stagger itself is unchanged.
  The UPML coefficient index is per cell and component on the scalar path, per z-vector
  and component on the f4vector path.
- 0004 (a5195df): a probe whose peak is below 1e-6 of the largest peak of its kind is
  measured against that floor (1e-6 of the largest peak), not against the largest peak.
  The hold counted from the signal length only and ignored excitation delays, and
  mode-match probes could never settle (both fixed here). "The port-signal settle test
  the post-processing applies anyway" is not part of openEMS: it was a check in the
  user's own post-processing.
- 0005 (99e20fd): the review was a further AI-assisted read-only review, not an
  independent one. The tuner clamp covered only the P+2.. candidates, not the start and
  start-2 (completed here). The packed-table size test could fire only through z
  padding, and then it also turned off the fused sweep (removed here). The excitation's
  APPLY_CURR saves a barrier per timestep only in the unfused plan. The arming rule
  catches only probes that are exactly zero: numerical precursors make a far probe
  non-zero shortly before its pulse arrives, so the hold must be longer than the longest
  one-way delay, and a probe that never sees a signal holds the run to its end (now with
  a notice).

